} {:db/id "q-333041/field-8/0d37364ecae1ec1056ebfbbec0cee2cfbe0a2384b337ef48c7ef780e4c6fab11" :answer/id #uuid "4a846b5f-1cf4-5a32-8bf1-9ca5325989e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333041/field-8/521de56a13c3839b22816f1406aeebb153a5c7c0003b45ef89e415366861c176" :answer/id #uuid "26bfacda-734b-5c7e-a45f-28b547428e8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333041/field-8/7458a52b2e4baf696c4042b4547293f99c1b6b34d97e4e509bc64e4e04198d3a" :answer/id #uuid "baeaa417-9b73-52ee-9aa0-ad77e64dc5db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}<0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333041/field-8" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1a727a4-2138-5f4a-9799-d44331e5cf42" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333041/field-8/7458a52b2e4baf696c4042b4547293f99c1b6b34d97e4e509bc64e4e04198d3a" :answer-field/choices ["q-333041/field-8/0d37364ecae1ec1056ebfbbec0cee2cfbe0a2384b337ef48c7ef780e4c6fab11" "q-333041/field-8/521de56a13c3839b22816f1406aeebb153a5c7c0003b45ef89e415366861c176" "q-333041/field-8/7458a52b2e4baf696c4042b4547293f99c1b6b34d97e4e509bc64e4e04198d3a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333041" :question/id #uuid "179f3ace-36b9-5ae3-b795-98edf695f28e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333041" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/56a90f9fb61640cc2eeca8e2f21f69381a1d53d008701ec457ade0fed7e54a64.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}." :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/1f166fad96cd30dbc0b9cf05fdabcccd26cd68e25fc26b91ae357d4015813614.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/cbb8268b959a09af3d0b57573ff08f45a2fb87f376d87f284df49cd5094412d9.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333041/field-1" "q-333041/field-2" "q-333041/field-3" "q-333041/field-4" "q-333041/field-5" "q-333041/field-6" "q-333041/field-7" "q-333041/field-8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13af6d27-628d-5bdb-b396-299d9cfd64e7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333041"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333328/field-1/b306ad6a3af7dc043f4c3e03da26a15440a9e9b19389783b2e1203bad6e37573" :answer/id #uuid "d01c3989-618a-5a01-b4c1-cb87d63a3282" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333328/field-1/da1e2ee83a948d87f5a1d96dda4b6d5e3c16439006d20546b9e74e8f37a8756c" :answer/id #uuid "2d6454bf-1f4c-5cf5-a626-2f9cb1ca3190" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333328/field-1/e819017e19dccac106b8bdd7a345917915a57ae6ae3646d73f366105b31b91f7" :answer/id #uuid "d55a8f6a-fcc3-56f2-94f1-48ea8eeb8a1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333328/field-1/570cbbc381f83341a867c9b2c248ecbbde4fe92928b4719fb7f3333aa7c824a3" :answer/id #uuid "2bea7779-bf35-50cb-b0bc-7fe3b04b00a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333328/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf72766f-efb2-56f1-9f62-b5c6eb661494" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333328/field-1/b306ad6a3af7dc043f4c3e03da26a15440a9e9b19389783b2e1203bad6e