:db/id "q-333328/field-10" :answer-field/id #uuid "12a267a7-f0b5-58c9-8512-94ef8d3a42c3" :answer-field/key "field-10" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333328/field-10/551dd1ea7e7bac6c6bd701b55450745ae020d94944976e90d8b3609af0e4d3aa" :answer-field/choices ["q-333328/field-10/d1d8c7c9f33610dc3c2ac8b5df654bfc924ee1361ff28cdb7bf3a860e40b944e" "q-333328/field-10/551dd1ea7e7bac6c6bd701b55450745ae020d94944976e90d8b3609af0e4d3aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333328/field-11/cb2748aca1c175694eed95d4e07947a1de83d69ab0f20fd05bd4c7de7a0eb195" :answer/id #uuid "4e14e26d-3b58-51ba-98d1-c8864c7852e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333328/field-11/2c9a7bdfdbfa12045e1d4ee8275d9021aea9665b523f9af3e4d21fe4520a23d2" :answer/id #uuid "08df8110-2fed-51be-b663-269bace79f90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333328/field-11/7e72f77b01549ada35dc8a8d936a2986901b482c620052f6c1151d25f19ff6f1" :answer/id #uuid "0eb70e61-32d4-5ae0-8dc9-fec32a77df87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1}<{\\lambda }_{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333328/field-11" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ead73b8-c687-58c4-9759-f43cc1b9ac1c" :answer-field/key "field-11" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333328/field-11/7e72f77b01549ada35dc8a8d936a2986901b482c620052f6c1151d25f19ff6f1" :answer-field/choices ["q-333328/field-11/cb2748aca1c175694eed95d4e07947a1de83d69ab0f20fd05bd4c7de7a0eb195" "q-333328/field-11/2c9a7bdfdbfa12045e1d4ee8275d9021aea9665b523f9af3e4d21fe4520a23d2" "q-333328/field-11/7e72f77b01549ada35dc8a8d936a2986901b482c620052f6c1151d25f19ff6f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333328" :question/id #uuid "f8cf237e-ffc7-539d-8c10-75ca847f73ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-333328" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/330e2a1dd9f7d2cbcadb61fe49e767d95ee76a2cb669a9917ef3f6333719b98f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$<0.$" :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/c675f557abede7c8f21eadf3fd65dbd522c5ee9ad0a4ed0a804b03f357c1dc3b.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069584/assets/a25c57a021e42b2fa78b71f9ab79f0676a16f7c4f1082af0e9502d4d63ed4e46.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are negative. The eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is the one closest to $0.$\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{2}$ as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior is ${\\lambda }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{1}<{\\lambda }_{2}<0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-333328/field-1" "q-333328/field-2" "q-333328/field-3" "q-333328/field-4" "q-333328/field-5" "q-333328/field-6" "q-333328/field-7" "q-333328/field-8" "q-333328/field-9" "q-333328/field-10" "q-333328/field-11"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13af6d27-628d-5bdb-b396-299